{
  "root_cause": "Escape keydown on a menu item triggers the dropdown hide method, but focus is not returned to the toggle after hiding. When the menu loses the .show class (display:none), the focused menu item becomes hidden and the browser shifts focus to the body, causing the focus assertion to fail.",
  "repair_strategy": "Modify the Dropdown.hide() method to restore focus to the toggle button (this._element) after completing the hide operation, ensuring focus returns to the activator when the dropdown is closed via Escape.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "js/src/dropdown.js",
      "sha256": "fc69a61b69ae1fa2c2bb923fdc393dd2742925e8af996376df5f98e38f8d810e",
      "before": "  hide() {\n    if (isDisabled(this._element) || !this._isShown(this._menu)) {\n      return\n    }\n\n    const relatedTarget = {\n      relatedTarget: this._element\n    }\n\n    this._completeHide(relatedTarget)\n  }",
      "after": "  hide() {\n    if (isDisabled(this._element) || !this._isShown(this._menu)) {\n      return\n    }\n\n    const relatedTarget = {\n      relatedTarget: this._element\n    }\n\n    this._completeHide(relatedTarget)\n    this._element.focus()\n  }"
  }
],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Pressing Escape while the actions menu is open will close the menu and move focus back to the 'Invoice actions' toggle button, meeting keyboard accessibility requirements for dropdown menus.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Focus may be restored to the toggle when the dropdown is dismissed by clicking outside or selecting a menu item, which could shift focus unexpectedly for some users.",
    "If the toggle button is disabled or detached at the time of hide, the focus call will be safely ignored by the browser."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Verify that pressing Escape with focus on any menu item results in aria-expanded='false' on the toggle and focus returning to the toggle.",
    "Verify that clicking outside the menu or selecting a menu item still closes the menu and does not cause JavaScript errors.",
    "Verify that the dropdown can still be opened via ArrowDown or click and that focus initially moves to the first menu item as before."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The journey steps:

Step 0: seek #actions-toggle

Step 1: press ArrowDown -> expects attribute aria-expanded="true" on #actions-toggle and focus on #action-send

Step 2: press Escape -> expects attribute aria-expanded="false" on #actions-toggle and focus back on #actions-toggle

Observed failure at step 2: After pressing Escape, they got focus still on something else? Actually assertion "focus" on #actions-toggle failed: observed false pass false. So focus didn't return to toggle.

In the observed after state for step 2, we see the HTML shows the menu still present? Let's examine.

Observed after for step 2 includes the whole body HTML showing the dropdown menu still visible? It says:

```
<div class=\"dropdown mb-3\">\n    <button class=\"btn btn-primary dropdown-toggle\" type=\"button\" id=\"actions-toggle\" data-bs-toggle=\"dropdown\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Invoice actions</button>\n    <ul class=\"dropdown-menu\" id=\"actions-menu\" aria-labelledby=\"actions-toggle\" style=\"\">\n      ...
```

So the menu is still present in DOM (it always is). But likely the dropdown is still open (has class 'show') causing focus to remain inside? However they said after Escape, the menu should close and focus return to toggle. In the observed state, they saw focus on body? Actually the "focus" in the state section shows focus on body (the whole body). That suggests after Escape, focus moved to body (maybe because the menu lost focus but didn't return to toggle). The expected behavior: after hiding dropdown, focus should return to the toggle button.

Looking at the bootstrap dropdown.js code: In hide() method, they call _completeHide(relatedTarget). In _completeHide, they remove CLASS_NAME_SHOW from menu and element, set aria-expanded false, etc. They also call EventHandler.trigger... but they do not manage focus.

In show() method, they call this._element.focus() and set aria-expanded true. In hide(), they do not restore focus to the toggle. Usually, for accessibility, when closing a dropdown, focus should return to the toggle. The bootstrap dropdown may not handle that; maybe they expect that the user pressed Escape while focus was on a menu item, and the dropdown's keydown handler (dataApiKeydownHandler) handles Escape by calling instance.hide() but does not return focus.

Let's examine dataApiKeydownHandler in dropdown.js:

```
static dataApiKeydownHandler(event) {
    // If not input/textarea:
    //  - And not a key in UP | DOWN | ESCAPE => not a dropdown command
    // If input/textarea && If key is other than ESCAPE
    //    - If key is not UP or DOWN => not a dropdown command
    //    - If trigger inside the menu => not a dropdown command

    const isInput = /input|textarea/i.test(event.target.tagName)
    const isEscapeEvent = event.key === ESCAPE_KEY
    const isUpOrDownEvent = [ARROW_UP_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key)

    if (!isInput && !(isUpOrDownEvent || isEscapeEvent)) {
      return
    }

    if (isInput && !isEscapeEvent) {
      // eslint-disable-next-line unicorn/no-lonely-if
      if (!isUpOrDownEvent || event.target.closest(SELECTOR_MENU)) {
        return
      }
    }

    const isActive = this.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)

    if (!isActive && isEscapeEvent) {
      return
    }

    event.preventDefault()
    event.stopPropagation()

    if (isDisabled(this)) {
      return
    }

    const getToggleButton = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]
    const instance = Dropdown.getOrCreateInstance(getToggleButton)

    if (isEscapeEvent) {
      instance.hide()
      return
    }

    if (isUpOrDownEvent) {
      instance.show()
      instance._selectMenuItem(event)
    }
  }
```

When Escape is pressed and the target is a menu item (like #action-send), isInput false, isEscapeEvent true, isUpOrDownEvent false => passes first if. Then isInput && !isEscapeEvent false (skip). Then const isActive = this.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW). Here "this" refers to event.target (the element that received the keydown). For a menu item, this.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW) will be false because the menu item itself does not have the show class; the menu (ul) has show. So isActive will be false. Since isEscapeEvent true and !isActive, the early return occurs: `if (!isActive && isEscapeEvent) { return; }`. Therefore Escape keydown on a menu item when dropdown is shown will be ignored! Wait but the observed keyEvents show: they had a keydown Escape on #action-send with defaultPrevented:true? Let's look at observed keyEvents:

```
"keyEvents":[{"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"Tab","target":"body","type":"keydown"},{"defaultPrevented":true,"key":"ArrowDown","target":"#actions-toggle","type":"keydown"},{"defaultPrevented":true,"key":"Escape","target":"#action-send","type":"keydown"}]
```

So Escape keydown on #action-send had defaultPrevented:true, meaning something prevented default. That suggests the handler did run and prevented default, but maybe didn't hide? Actually they called instance.hide() which should hide. Yet after that, focus didn't return.

But the condition `if (!isActive && isEscapeEvent) { return; }` would cause early return if the target (menu item) does not have class show. However they still got defaultPrevented true, meaning the handler didn't early return. Let's double-check: In dataApiKeydownHandler, `this` refers to the element that the listener was attached to. The listener is attached to SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE and SELECTOR_MENU. Indeed they register:

```
EventHandler.on(document, EVENT_KEYDOWN_DATA_API, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE, Dropdown.dataApiKeydownHandler)
EventHandler.on(document, EVENT_KEYDOWN_DATA_API, SELECTOR_MENU, Dropdown.dataApiKeydownHandler)
```

So when Escape is pressed while focus is on #action-send (which is inside the menu), the listener on SELECTOR_MENU will fire, with `this` being the menu ul (#actions-menu). Because the event delegated from document to the menu element. So `this` is the menu element, which does have class 'show' when open. Therefore isActive will be true (since menu has show). So the early return won't happen. Good.

Thus Escape keydown on menu triggers handler on the menu element, calls instance.hide() (on the toggle). Then after hide, focus should be returned? Not automatically.

Now why focus didn't return to toggle? Possibly because after hide, the focus remains on the menu item that had focus before hiding (since the menu item is still in DOM but hidden via removal of show class; however it's still focusable and maybe retains focus). The observed state shows focus on body (maybe because the menu item got removed from tab order due to being hidden? Actually the menu item is still in DOM but its parent ul lost show class; but the menu item itself is still focusable unless we add tabindex=-1 or something. However the browser might have moved focus to body because the focused element became invisible or detached? The spec says if an element becomes hidden (display none) or removed, focus may shift. But here they just remove show class from menu; the menu is still visible? Actually the dropdown-menu with class show makes it display:block (via Bootstrap CSS). Without show, it's hidden via display:none? Let's check: In Bootstrap, .dropdown-menu has position: absolute; inset: 0; margin:0; list-style:none; etc. The .show class likely sets display: block. So removing show hides it (display:none). When an element with display:none is focused, the browser may move focus elsewhere (to body). That matches observed: focus on body.

Thus we need to return focus to the toggle after hiding. The fix: In Dropdown.hide() or _completeHide, after hiding, we should focus the toggle button (this._element). However we must ensure we don't break other use cases where focus should stay elsewhere (like clicking outside). But for Escape key, we want to return focus.

Alternatively, we could modify the dataApiKeydownHandler to after calling instance.hide(), also focus the toggle. But better to handle in hide method.

Let's look at hide method:

```
hide() {
    if (isDisabled(this._element) || !this._isShown(this._menu)) {
      return
    }

    const relatedTarget = {
      relatedTarget: this._element
    }

    this._completeHide(relatedTarget)
}
```

_completeHide does the hiding but does not manage focus.

We can add focus restoration in hide after _completeHide, or inside _completeHide after removing show class.

But note: hide() may be called from other contexts (click outside, etc.) where we might not want to steal focus. However the spec for dropdowns: When dismissing dropdown via Escape, focus should return to the toggle. When dismissing via click outside, maybe focus should stay where it was (or go to body). But many implementations return focus to toggle on any dismiss. Let's check Bootstrap's behavior: I think Bootstrap 5 dropdown does not manage focus on hide; it relies on the browser's default behavior. However for accessibility, they recommend adding focus management. The issue here is that Escape key press on menu item results in focus loss. So we need to fix.

We'll edit the hide method to focus the toggle after hiding, but only if the hiding was triggered via Escape? Could detect via relatedTarget? Not passed. Simpler: always focus toggle after hide. This may cause focus to jump when clicking outside, but maybe acceptable. However we need to be careful not to break other functionality like clicking a menu item (which should not hide? Actually clicking a menu item hides via clearMenus on click event, which calls _completeHide via relatedTarget.clickEvent? Let's see clearMenus: on click, they call context._completeHide(relatedTarget). That does not focus toggle. So if we add focus to hide method, clicking a menu item will also cause focus to return to toggle after hiding, which may be undesirable because after clicking a menu item, you might want focus to stay where it was (maybe on the item). However the menu item is hidden after click, so focus would be lost anyway. Many designs move focus to toggle after clicking a menu item. Might be okay.

